40 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551225 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551225 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551225 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186550478 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551225 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f757e78b-6af6-5637-affb-8a450f17cc85" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551228 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186550402 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551228 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186550428 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551228 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186550773 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551228 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186550890 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551228 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186551000 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551228 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551228 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551228 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186550428 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551228 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fdd5f643-5dc8-5fd7-924a-fad98f3489b8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551243 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551128 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551243 #edb/ref 1069 "q-135555" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551243 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551243 #edb/ref 1071 "In a particular country, there are two groups of people: those who live in the cities and those who live in the countryside. A study has shown that each decade \n\n- $12%$ of people who lived in the cities relocate to the countryside, and\n\n- $28%$ of people who lived in the countryside relocate to the cities.\n\nWhich of the following tables describes how these people change their preferences each year?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551243 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1cf7f6b8-f438-52c0-a338-61f2ad7fa03e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551243 #edb/ref 1074 "We are given the following situation for each decade: \n\n- $12%$ of people who live in the cities relocate to the countryside. This implies that $88%$ of them remain living in the cities.\n\n- $28%$ of people who live in the countryside relocate to the cities. This implies that $72%$ of them remain living in the countryside.\n\nWriting this information in tabular form, we obtain the following:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"2\">From:</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"60\"><b>Cities</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"60\"><b>Countryside</b></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">To:</td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"60\"><b>Cities</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.88$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.28$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"60\"><b>Countryside</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.12$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.72$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nNote that we can write a corresponding stochastic matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0.88 & 0.28 \\\\ 0.12 & 0.72\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551261 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551184 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551261 #edb/ref 1069 "q-35847" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551261 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551261 #edb/ref 1071 "A certain company produces $2$ types of flour, flour $A$ and flour $B.$ The following table describes how the number of consumers changes between both types of flour each month:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"2\">From:</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Flour $A$</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Flour $B$</b></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">To:</td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Flour $A$</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Flour $B$</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.7$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.4$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nThat is, $70%$ of flour $A$ customers switch to flour $B$ each month, and $60%$ of flour $B$ customers change to flour $A.$\n\nCurrently, $6\\,000$ customers use flour $A,$ while $4\\,000$ customers use flour $B.$ How many customers will use flour $A$ in one month? You may assume that the total number of customers doesn't change." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551261 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "34952883-ed67-58e1-83a4-6ee369a6dde8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551261 #edb/ref 1074 "From the table, we can build the corresponding stochastic matrix: \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.3 & 0.6 \\\\ 0.7 & 0.4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vector describing the number of customers for each type of flour is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}6\\,000 \\\\ 4\\,000\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nDividing the vector above by the total number of customers $(10\\,000),$ we get the stochastic vector of the initial state:\n\n$$\n{x}_{0}=\\frac{1}{10\\,000}[\\begin{aligned}6\\,000 \\\\ 4\\,000\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.6 \\\\ 0.4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo find the state vector ${x}_{1}$ after one year, we apply one step of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=P{x}_{0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.3 & 0.6 \\\\ 0.7 & 0.4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.6 \\\\ 0.4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.42 \\\\ 0.58\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after one month, $42%$ of customers will use flour $A.$ Computing $42%$ of $10\\,000,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n10\\,000\\cdot 0.42=4\\,200.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $4\\,200$ customers will use flour $A$ in one month." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551279 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551151 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551279 #edb/ref 1069 "q-135481" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551279 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551279 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following are stochastic vectors?\n\n$$\nu=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0.5\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}-0.5 \\\\ 1.5\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0.1 \\\\ 0.9\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551279 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "55574236-41f2-5d82-abc0-f140064784e6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551279 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a stochastic vector is a vector with nonnegative entries that add up to $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our vectors in turn.\n\n- Vector $u$ is *not*  a stochastic vector since the entries do not add up to one:\n\n$$\n0+0.5=0.5\\ne 1\n$$\n\n- Vector $v$ is *not* a stochastic vector since it contains negative entries.\n\n- Vector $w$ is a stochastic vector. Indeed, \n\n- its entries are all nonnegative, and\n\n- the entries add up to one:\n\n$$\n0+0.1+0.9=1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$w$ only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551290 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551158 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551290 #edb/ref 1069 "q-135486" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551290 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551290 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following are stochastic matrices?\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}0.1 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.9 & 0.3\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}0.45 & 0.35 \\\\ 0.25 & 0.65\\end{aligned}],\\,W=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551290 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "718b908f-f156-56c4-954e-a24b1b0d0bf9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551290 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a stochastic vector is a vector with nonnegative entries that add up to $1.$ A matrix is stochastic if all its columns are stochastic vectors.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices in turn.\n\n- Matrix $U$ is a stochastic matrix. Indeed, each column is a stochastic vector (the corresponding entries are nonnegative and add up to one): \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}0.1 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.9 & 0.3\\end{aligned}]\\,\\begin{aligned}0.1+0.9=1 \\\\ 0.7+0.3=1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Matrix $V$ is *not* a stochastic matrix since its first column is not a stochastic vector (the corresponding entries don't add up to one): \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0.45 & 0.35 \\\\ 0.25 & 0.65\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Matrix $W$  *not* a stochastic matrix since its second column is not a stochastic vector (it contains negative entries):\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$U$ only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551295 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551146 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551295 #edb/ref 1069 "q-135564" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551295 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551295 #edb/ref 1071 "There are three supermarket chains in a particular city: chain $A,$ chain $B,$ and chain $C.$ A study has shown that, each month\n\n- from the customers that prefer chain $A,$ $5%$ change to chain $B$ and $15%$ change to chain $C,$\n\n- from the customers that prefer chain $B,$ $25%$ change to chain $A$ and $25%$ change to chain $C,$\n\n- from the customers that prefer chain $C,$ $15%$ change to chain $A$ and $35%$ change to chain $B.$\n\nThe stochastic matrix that describes this situation is given below. What is the value of $a+b+c?$\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.8 & a & 0.15 \\\\ b & 0.5 & 0.35 \\\\ 0.15 & 0.25 & c\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551295 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7d2bfda5-1f22-5eca-90c5-42502b97859c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551295 #edb/ref 1074 "We are given the following situation for each month:\n\n- From the customers that prefer chain $A,$ $5%$ change to chain $B$ and $15%$ change to chain $C.$ This implies that $80%$ of them continue to prefer chain $A.$\n\n- From the customers that prefer chain $B,$ $25%$ change to chain $A$ and $25%$ change to chain $C.$ This implies that $50%$ of them continue to prefer chain $B.$\n\n- From the customers that prefer chain $C,$ $15%$ change to chain $A$ and $35%$ change to chain $B.$ This implies that $50%$ of them continue to prefer chain $C.$\n\nWriting this information in tabular form, we obtain the following:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\">From:</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Chain A</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Chain B</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Chain C</b></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"3\">To:</td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Chain A</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.8$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.25$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.15$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Chain B</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.05$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.5$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.35$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Chain C</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.15$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.25$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.5$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nWe can write the corresponding stochastic matrix as follows:\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.8 & 0.25 & 0.15 \\\\ 0.05 & 0.5 & 0.35 \\\\ 0.15 & 0.25 & 0.5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=0.25+0.05+0.5=0.8.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551298 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551228 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551298 #edb/ref 1069 "q-135449" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551298 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551298 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.15 & 0.45 \\\\ 0.85 & 0.55\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}0.2 \\\\ 0.8\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \n\nConsider a Markov chain with the stochastic matrix $P$ and the initial state vector ${x}_{0}$ given above. Calculate the state vector ${x}_{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551298 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8271e0f5-ad00-5357-b8f4-39e3fffd35ad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551298 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the state vector ${x}_{2},$ we apply two steps of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=P{x}_{0},\\,{x}_{2}=P{x}_{1}\n$$\n\nAfter the first step, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.15 & 0.45 \\\\ 0.85 & 0.55\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.2 \\\\ 0.8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.39 \\\\ 0.61\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, after the second step, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =P{x}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.15 & 0.45 \\\\ 0.85 & 0.55\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.39 \\\\ 0.61\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.333 \\\\ 0.667\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551321 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551225 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551321 #edb/ref 1069 "q-135453" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551321 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551321 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.35 & 0.15 \\\\ 0.65 & 0.85\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}0.3 \\\\ 0.7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \n\nConsider a Markov chain with the stochastic matrix $P$ and the initial state vector ${x}_{0}$ given above. Calculate the state vector ${x}_{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551321 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "aad00a02-7311-5843-ab16-bebead54d45c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551321 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the state vector ${x}_{2},$ we apply two steps of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=P{x}_{0},\\,{x}_{2}=P{x}_{1}\n$$\n\nAfter the first step, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.35 & 0.15 \\\\ 0.65 & 0.85\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.3 \\\\ 0.7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.21 \\\\ 0.79\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, after the second step, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =P{x}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.35 & 0.15 \\\\ 0.65 & 0.85\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.21 \\\\ 0.79\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.192 \\\\ 0.808\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551325 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551201 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551325 #edb/ref 1069 "q-135463" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551325 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551325 #edb/ref 1071 "A certain store sells three types of instant soups: chicken, meat, and vegetable. The following table describes how the number of customers changes between the three types of instant soups each month:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\">From:</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"60\"><b>Chicken</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"60\"><b>Meat</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"60\"><b>Vegetable</b></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"3\">To:</td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"60\"><b>Chicken</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.4$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"60\"><b>Meat</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.4$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"60\"><b>Vegetable</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.4$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.5$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nFor example, $30%$ of chicken soup customers switch to vegetable each month, and $20%$ of vegetable customers switch to meat.\n\nCurrently, $400$ customers buy chicken soup, $400$ customers buy meat soup, and $200$ customers buy vegetable soup. How many customers will purchase vegetable instant soup in one month? You may assume that the total number of customers doesn't change." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551325 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "af97554b-6ce7-555f-8ebb-05f560ef6317" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551325 #edb/ref 1074 "From the table, we can build the corresponding stochastic matrix: \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.4 & 0.2 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.4 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.4 & 0.5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vector describing the number of customers for each type of instant soup is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}400 \\\\ 400 \\\\ 200\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nDividing the vector above by the total number of customers $(1\\,000),$ we get the stochastic vector of the initial state:\n\n$$\n{x}_{0}=\\frac{1}{1\\,000}[\\begin{aligned}400 \\\\ 400 \\\\ 200\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.4 \\\\ 0.4 \\\\ 0.2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo find the state vector ${x}_{1}$ after one month, we apply one step of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=P{x}_{0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.4 & 0.2 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.4 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.4 & 0.5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.4 \\\\ 0.4 \\\\ 0.2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.3 \\\\ 0.32 \\\\ 0.38\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after one month, $38%$ of customers will buy vegetable soup. Computing $38%$ of $1\\,000,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n1\\,000\\cdot 0.38=380.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $380$ customers will buy vegetable instant soup in one month." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
